--
-- WHY A BACKFILL AND NOT MORE BILLS
-- The record's thinness on these issues was never a shortage of legislation. Every one of
-- these 119 roll calls is already here and each already carries curated issue mappings;
-- what was missing was people. The rolls were seeded a handful of hand-listed voters at
-- a time, so a member could hold a stated position on an issue, have cast a recorded
-- vote on six mapped measures under it, and still render as though they had never acted.
-- One more bill would not have fixed that for a single one of them.
--
-- WHERE THE CELLS COME FROM
-- clerk.house.gov/evs/<year>/roll<NNN>.xml — for most of these rolls, the document
-- vr_rollcalls.source_url already cites. Each was identity-verified before a single cell
-- was taken from it: rollcall-num, congress, action-date,
-- all four totals-by-vote counts against the recorded votes actually listed, the measure
-- number (or its parent's, for amendments), recorded-vote count equal to the totals sum,
-- and a Bioguide on every recorded vote. 120 rolls were checked; 0 were dropped whole.
-- Nothing here is inferred from a party, a cosponsorship, a neighbouring vote or a
-- pattern: a member absent from the Clerk's recorded-vote list
-- gets no row, and an unresolvable Bioguide is skipped and counted, never guessed.
--
-- is_party IS RECOMPUTED, NOT INHERITED
-- Each cell's is_party is derived from the full chamber tally in the same document the
-- vote was read from — the member's own party's yea/nay split on that roll — so it
-- describes where they stood among their colleagues on that vote and nothing else. It
-- is a stored fact about the roll, not a score, and nothing in the product ranks on it.
--
-- 17 CELLS THIS MIGRATION REFUSES TO TOUCH
-- 5521 cells already stored on these rolls were re-read against the Clerk and agree.
